.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt PS 1
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ps
.Nd list services from the svc: scheme
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl -json
.Nm
.Fl -selftest
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the directory
.Pa svc:
and each
.Pa svc:/<name>
status record, and prints one line per service:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NAME STATE RESTARTS
.Ed
.Pp
The state is one of
.Ql stopped ,
.Ql starting ,
.Ql running ,
.Ql restarting ,
.Ql failed
or
.Ql gave-up .
.Pp
.Fl -selftest
reads every status record, checks that the directory is non-empty and every
line parses, and prints
.Dq ps: selftest ok .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -json
Print one NDJSON object per service instead of the aligned human lines.
The object has the fixed envelope
.Ql schema:1
and
.Ql kind:"process"
followed by
.Ql pid ,
.Ql ppid ,
.Ql uid ,
.Ql gid ,
.Ql state ,
.Ql name ,
.Ql command ,
.Ql threads
and
.Ql rss_bytes .
The current
.Pa svc:
source provides only the service name and state; the process fields it does
not provide are
.Ql null ,
never invented values.
There is no wrapper array, header or colour in this mode.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A usage error exits with status 2.
A missing or malformed
.Pa svc:
record exits with status 1; with
.Fl -json
the error is one
.Ql kind:"error"
object on standard error, and a partial listing continues.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ps --json
{"schema":1,"kind":"process","pid":null,"ppid":null,"uid":null,"gid":null,"state":"running","name":"logd","command":null,"threads":null,"rss_bytes":null}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr svcctl 1 ,
.Xr init 8
.Sh STANDARDS
The output is a RavenBSD service view, not the process table of a POSIX
.Nm
utility.
